# Muxel secrets.
#
# There are none. That is not an omission, it is the design: a deployment has
# nothing to be given, so this file lists nothing and the deploy form asks you
# for nothing. Press the button and your Worker is running.
#
# The deploy flow reads this file to work out what to ask for, and it makes
# every name it finds a required field. A single box here would stop someone
# who has just pressed a deploy button and ask them to invent a password for a
# thing that does not exist yet. Your deployment invents a far better one
# itself, and shows it to you on its own front page the first time you open it.
#
# Everything Muxel can be given — a console bot in Telegram, a key for a model
# from another provider, live web search, reading a document as data — is added
# afterwards, from the Worker's own Settings, Variables and Secrets, by someone
# who by then knows what it is for.
#
# Copy this file to .dev.vars for local development. Never commit real values:
# .dev.vars is ignored by git for that reason.
#
# The optional settings, for reference. None of them belong on the deploy form.
#
#   CONSOLE_KEY        Choose your own console key instead of the issued one,
#                      at least 16 characters. Setting it also ends every
#                      session the previous key opened, which is how you take
#                      back a key that leaked.
#   ADMIN_BOT_TOKEN    A Telegram bot to run the console from, and the account
#   OWNER_TELEGRAM_ID  allowed to use it. Both, or neither.
#   CF_API_TOKEN       Account Analytics: Read, so the console can show your
#                      real Cloudflare usage rather than Muxel's own estimate.
